@page "/learn/wasm-hosted-services"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Background services in the browser · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<style>
    .learn .tab.idle { background: #24231f; color: var(--soft); }
</style>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Background services in the browser</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your bell checks for order updates. Does it keep checking when you change pages?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> in the browser, services you register with <code>AddHostedService</code> now
        start on their own, just like on a server. In .NET 10 you could write the <code>AddHostedService</code> line,
        but the service never ran, and nothing told you.
    </p>

    <p>
        Say you want a bell on the <i>Orders</i> page that counts order updates, checking the server every 3
        seconds. The obvious way is a timer in the bell:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The bell, with its own timer</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="err">readonly PeriodicTimer timer = new(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3));</span>

protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
{
    while (await timer.WaitForNextTickAsync()) await CheckAsync();
}

<span class="err">public void Dispose() =&gt; timer.Dispose();</span></pre>
    </div>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A bell with its own timer" Url="demo/hosted-services/orders/net10"
                Label="The bell runs its own timer" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Wait a few seconds for the bell to count some updates. Click <b>Customers</b>, then <b>Orders</b>: the
            bell is back at <b>0</b>. (The table under the bell comes up below.)
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The page owns the timer</h2>
    <p>The timer lives in the bell, and the bell lives in the page:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What lives how long in the browser</span>
        <pre class="snip">Browser tab
└─ WebAssembly runtime            <span class="ok">✓ keeps running as you change pages</span>
   ├─ Orders page
   │  └─ PollingBell
   │     └─ PeriodicTimer         <span class="err">disposed when you leave Orders</span>
   └─ services                    <span class="ok">✓ live as long as the runtime</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Leave Orders and the bell is thrown away, with its timer and its count. Come back and a new bell starts from
        zero. The checking belongs in a service, which lives as long as the app. The usual way to run one is a hosted
        service: a <code>BackgroundService</code> that the app starts and keeps running. You register it like this:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Program.cs, in the WebAssembly project</span>
        <pre class="snip">builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;();
builder.Services.AddHostedService(sp =&gt; sp.GetRequiredService&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;());

await builder.Build().RunAsync();   <span class="err">// .NET 10: never starts it</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>Here's what that does on .NET 10:</p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 2 · .NET 10: the app starts, and you visit three pages</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h bad">.NET 10</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li>AddHostedService(watcher) <span class="done" style="--d:0.6s">✓ compiles</span></li>
                <li>start the watcher <span class="wait" style="--d:1.5s">… nothing happens</span></li>
                <li>show the page <span class="done" style="--d:2.7s">✓</span></li>
                <li class="never" style="--d:3.6s">check for updates every 3 s</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tabs">
                <div class="tab idle" style="--d:4.2s"><b>Orders</b><span>bell: 0</span></div>
                <div class="tab idle" style="--d:4.8s"><b>Customers</b><span>not checking</span></div>
                <div class="tab idle" style="--d:5.4s"><b>Orders again</b><span>bell: 0</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict bad" style="--d:6s">✗ the watcher never runs. No error, no warning.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            The line compiles (on .NET 10 the WebAssembly project needs the
            <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions</code> package for that), then does nothing. The app never starts the
            watcher, so the bell sits at 0 on every page.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <p>
        The table under the bell in Fig. 1 shows the same thing, recorded on a real .NET 10 app with three watchers:
        the bell's own timer (reset when you left), <code>AddHostedService</code> (stayed at 0), and one started by
        hand in <code>Program.cs</code> (kept counting). That last one is the .NET 10 workaround, shown further down.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Upgrade: the same code now runs</h2>
    <p>
        Keep the hosted service. On .NET 11 the WebAssembly app starts it, just as a server app would. Here's the
        code again, with the parts that matter:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The watcher and its registration</span>
        <pre class="snip">public sealed class OrderWatcher(HttpClient http) : <span class="hl">BackgroundService</span> { ... }

<span class="dim">// Program.cs, in the WebAssembly project</span>
builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;();
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddHostedService(sp =&gt; sp.GetRequiredService&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;());</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Now the bell doesn't check anything. It just listens to the watcher. <code>AddSingleton</code> gives every
        bell the same watcher, and the lambda tells the app to start that one. If you wrote
        <code>AddHostedService&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;()</code> instead, you'd get two watchers: the app would start one,
        and the bells would listen to the other, which never checks.
    </p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 3 · .NET 11: the same app, the same three pages</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h ok">.NET 11</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li>AddHostedService(watcher) <span class="done" style="--d:0.6s">✓ compiles</span></li>
                <li>start the watcher <span class="done" style="--d:1.5s">✓ started</span></li>
                <li>show the page <span class="done" style="--d:2.7s">✓</span></li>
                <li>check for updates every 3 s <span class="done" style="--d:3.9s">✓ running</span></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tabs">
                <div class="tab" style="--d:4.2s"><b>Orders</b><span>bell counts up</span></div>
                <div class="tab" style="--d:4.8s"><b>Customers</b><span>still checking</span></div>
                <div class="tab" style="--d:5.4s"><b>Orders again</b><span>count carried on</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict ok" style="--d:6s">✓ the app starts the watcher before the first page.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            Same code. This time the watcher starts with the app and keeps checking while you change pages.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <DemoFigure Number="4" Title="The same pages, with a hosted watcher" Url="demo/hosted-services/orders"
                Label=".NET 11: AddHostedService">
        <Caption>
            Wait for the bell to count some updates. Click <b>Customers</b>, then <b>Orders</b>: the count didn't
            reset. It kept going while you were away.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the code:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Client/Program.cs" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>The registration: one watcher, started by the app.</Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Client/HostedServices/OrderWatcher.cs" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>
            A normal <code>BackgroundService</code>, the same kind you'd use on a server. It checks every 3 seconds
            until the tab closes.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="7" Path="Client/HostedServices/OrderBell.razor" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>
            The bell from Fig. 4. It only listens, so it can come and go without stopping the checks. Nothing here is
            new. The highlights show what replaced the timer.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="8" Path="Client/HostedServices/PollingBell.razor" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>
            For comparison, the bell from Fig. 1. Its timer starts with the bell, and <code>Dispose</code> stops it.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Check from the page, or start it yourself</h2>
    <p>
        The table in Fig. 1 came from a .NET 10 app that tried three ways at once. Each one ticked once a second, faster than the bell, to make the recording quick. The
        numbers are its ticks after 5 seconds on Orders, then on Customers, then back on Orders.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three watchers in one .NET 10 app, and their ticks</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">PeriodicTimer in the component</pre>
                <span class="why">5, then gone on Customers, then back to 1</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ starts again from nothing</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">builder.Services
  .AddHostedService(...);</pre>
                <span class="why">won't compile without the Hosting.Abstractions package; with it, stays at 0</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ never starts, with no warning</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">var host = builder.Build();
_ = watcher.StartAsync(
    CancellationToken.None);
await host.RunAsync();</pre>
                <span class="why">the "Started by hand" row: 5, 8, 10</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ keeps counting across pages</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Starting it yourself worked, but only for your own services. A hosted service that came from a library, like
        OpenTelemetry's, still never started.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The app starts them</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the WebAssembly app calls <code>StartAsync</code> on every hosted service, and waits for it, before
        it shows anything. The hosting types come with the WebAssembly SDK, so there's no package to add.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It starts before the first render.</b> So keep <code>StartAsync</code> quick. A <code>BackgroundService</code> is fine: its <code>StartAsync</code> returns as soon as your <code>ExecuteAsync</code> starts waiting on something.</li>
        <li><b>One thread.</b> In the browser, .NET runs on a single thread. Waiting, like this check, is fine. A long calculation freezes the page.</li>
        <li><b>One copy per tab.</b> It starts with the first WebAssembly component on the page. A reload or a new tab starts a fresh copy. Closing the tab ends it without calling <code>StopAsync</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Sometimes a component survives a page change.</b> When you follow a link, Blazor usually swaps in the new page without a full reload. If the new page has the same WebAssembly component in the same place, Blazor keeps it. So if you put the bell from Fig. 1 on every page, its timer would keep going. It would still stop on any page without the bell.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
